234:The rest chapter — integration cost, aftermath, and the series' third staged birthday, deliberately the smallest and deliberately the last inside an institution's walls. The destabilization is paid on-page per the B3 Ch14 constraint and managed with the B4-established discipline: recovery days banked, the circle warned, nothing evaluable scheduled — and it comes anyway, real: the Anchor-adjacent component *pulls* like nothing before it, wanting to fix points in ordinary rooms, doorways going subtly wrong at the edges of his attention until he learns its idle state; the Storm-adjacent framework, heaviest neighbor, resettles twice. Karis documents the comparative data. Eighth acquisition, second Silver-or-above in two books; her finding, delivered carefully: the architecture is accelerating into heavier sources, and the recovery arithmetic is holding — "for now" entered into the record at her insistence.
247:Seln talks for longer than he has talked in three books, in the flattest voice the book gives anyone, because the content does not need help: how it started as insurance (an operative's habit; every posting, every anomaly, duplicated and kept); how it stopped being insurance the year he noticed the anomalies had a *direction* — inconvenient abilities corrected downward, politically useful ones left alone, across postings, across decades of file-dates, across more years than any conspiracy of the living could span; how he walked away from the job in the only way his trade allows (by staying in it, and keeping); and why now — Chapter 9's silence, Chapter 11's paper, Chapter 13's road, given four sentences total. The finding, stated once, plainly, for the record the annex will never keep: **the Guilds Compact has been falsifying Path classifications for two hundred years — at Kindling, through the Arbiter protocol itself, via a modification so old that no current official knows it is there.** The room takes it the way the book has earned: Karis reaching for the memoranda with the expression she reserves for primary sources; Brom completely still; Lira's single question — "How many?" — and Seln's answer — "I stopped estimating. Estimating felt like filing." 
365:The last chapter — the ledger, the file, and the door into Arc 3, in the series' Chapter 24 register: small leavings and stayings, each heavier than its size. The first true edge-territories camp: a switchback shelf above an unnamed valley, firelight, the found family's roles running on new terrain with old grammar — Brom's quiet competence now aimed at load-balancing a cart instead of acquiring dinner twice over; Karis indexing her three boxes into a working field-archive; Lira mapping tomorrow's route like a bracket sheet; Seln gone for an hour and back with the lay of every approach, unasked; Ephram's absence at the fire noted once, without grief — he stayed, and staying was right for him, and the family's arithmetic holds him anyway. The ledger of the year, in its established forms. The Power Log inventory, in Hesk's satchel, by firelight, shown complete: **nine confirmed fragments** — Wind-adjacent (Lira), Pressure-adjacent (Feryn), Iron-adjacent (Brom), Compression-adjacent (Reydan), Ember-adjacent (Karis), Shadow-adjacent (Seln), Storm-adjacent (Daeva), Anchor-adjacent (unnamed), Blade-adjacent (Ephram) — nine integrated abilities, each with a name beside it except the one the system sent namelessly, and the anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, turned over once, closed with the standing three words: *Still open. Still real. Patience.* One log line for the old promises: "Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. The road east is long. Good." Letters, held for the next westbound courier whenever one exists: to Hesk (*"Eighteen, unenrolled, and past the Line. The shape you said to mind — I minded it. The tools fit the road. — C."*), to Vell, because her ledger holds his first honest record and the resignation belongs in it.
