391:- [ ] **The internal arc staged honestly at both poles:** *I can work within the system* written in the log, in confidence, with three books of receipts, Chapter 2; defended at council against Lira's honest challenge, Chapter 9; cracked on the Ostrand road, Chapter 13 ("the room was never the system"); landed intellectually in the annex, Chapter 16 ("It isn't broken. It's *maintained*."); landed finally, without triumphalism, at the fire, Chapter 24. The book's structural discipline carries the argument mechanically: the defense wins every argument (Ch8's sustained-validity, Ch10's Jent admission, Ch18's masterwork closing) and the machine proceeds anyway — the reader is never allowed to believe a better brief would have saved them, because the corridor was never an argument. And the walkout is earned by pricing, not by rhetoric: every stake is built (Ch4), lived in (Ch10, Ch19), and paid on the page (Ch21) — the book ends with the companions owning nothing the system gave them except each other and the truth, which is the Arc 2 exit the whole arc was for.
   190	The first off-channel action — Vastin's Book 5 warning materializing on the page, staged with the series' institutional precision: nothing illegal happens, which is what makes it the book's most frightening chapter to date. Four practitioners arrive at Halcenvane's gate bearing an "escort order" for the enrollee's transfer to a registry facility for *pre-evaluation assessment* — ahead of the proceedings' schedule, under a seal Bracken cannot match to any issuing office, on paper that is genuine registry stock. Withrow meets them at the gate herself with counsel and refuses on procedure: the proceedings' schedule is the schedule; no facility transfer is contemplated; the order's issuing authority is unidentifiable. The four accept the refusal without argument — which everyone present understands was itself the probe: measuring whether the paper would work, at no cost if it didn't — and leave.
   191	
   192	Cael watches the whole exchange from the gallery above the gate, and the chapter runs his observation discipline at full depth on the one of the four who never speaks: a compact, unhurried woman who spends the entire standoff positioned at the geometric point commanding every exit, laying — he realizes slowly, cataloguing — *nothing*. No declaration, no architecture, no tell. Just placement. The observation notebook gets its first Anchor Path entry that night, built from inference alone: containment craft, fixed-point architecture, the Path that holds people; deployed here as pure positioning, which means discipline of a grade he has seen exactly twice — Seln, and Vastin. Seln confirms the read and adds the fact that matters, in one flat sentence: "Anchor specialists at that grade work enforcement. Enforcement doesn't do assessment escorts. Whoever wrote that paper is done assessing."
   193	
   194	*Close on:* the inner circle in the records hall, the escort order's copy on the table, and Seln looking at it longer than anyone — the chapter's last image held on him, because the reader (tracking his Chapter 9 window) can see what the companions can't yet: a man watching the machine he carries the blueprints for begin to move, and running out of reasons. The log: "Vastin said the people who want this to stop wouldn't use official channels much longer. Today the unofficial channel knocked at the gate, politely, in genuine paper. It'll knock once more somewhere without a Withrow in front of it. Whatever we're going to do, the schedule isn't ours anymore."
