   198	**Chapter 12 — The Keeper**
   199	~4,600 words
   200	
   201	The hinge — Seln's decision completing, staged in the register Book 4 built for him: visible entirely in behavior, confessed never. The chapter opens on his window and stays there longer than the series has ever stayed: the escort order's forensics (he traced the paper stock; he knows which supply chain it walked out of; he has *run* operations on that stock), the pre-restriction inventory, the four grades of pressure he can name that come next — and against all of it, the boy's Chapter 9 sentence, quoted in Seln's own memory with professional distaste: *the text still works.* The window walks his logic one last time — the operative's catechism, fifteen years old: insurance is only insurance while it stays sealed; the moment the case opens, the man holding it stops being a keeper and becomes a witness, and witnesses do not retire — and then watches the logic lose, without drama, the way everything about Seln happens: he takes the case down. He does not open it. He carries it, for the first time in nineteen postings, out of his quarters and across the campus, and requests five minutes of Cael's evening.
   202	
   203	The conversation is four sentences long and the book stages it complete. Seln: "You said the text still works. I need to show you whose text it is. Not here — all six of us, somewhere I choose, this week." Cael, who has spent four books reading this man and knows the case by sight the way the reader does: "You're sure." Seln: "No. That's how you'll know it's true."
   204	
   205	*Close on:* the arrangements, in tradecraft — the venue Seln selects (a records annex he controls the sightlines of), the schedule gap he manufactures, the null report he files that week shown one last time in the old pattern, true and complete and empty — and one paragraph of Havel, on the proceedings' records rotation at Ostrand, logging exhibits late at night, adding a sixth entry to the private note; carrier intact, unknowing, converging. The chapter ends the night before the annex, on Cael's log: "Fifteen years he's kept whatever it is. He's spending it on us. Whatever's in that case, it's about to become the most expensive thing anyone has ever handed me. Sleep. Read in the morning."
