   211	
   212	The interception — the second off-channel action, and the series' Chapter 13 acquisition tradition (B2, B3, B4) honored a fourth time, at the highest stakes it has ever run. The annex meeting is set; the proceedings' schedule intervenes first — a documentary session at Ostrand requiring the enrollee's presence — and the delegation coach takes the river road it has taken all term. The ambush is staged with the book's off-channel signature: no uniforms, no declarations of authority, no paper at all this time — six practitioners, road discipline, and the unhurried Anchor specialist from the gate laying her lattice across the road's cut *before* the coach enters it, fixed points blooming in a geometry Cael's Iron-adjacent read maps in real time and cannot find the exit of. The purpose is transparent and the chapter states it coldly: not assault — *custody*. A detention that will be paperwork by the time anyone asks, executed where no Withrow stands.
   213	
   214	The fight is the book's first full choreography and the found family's — five practitioners plus Seln against six, on a road, for keeps. The chapter runs it in the series' register: Lira's Silver-honed speed breaking the perimeter's timing, Brom pricing the strike team's contact points, Karis's improvised-style lattice-work jamming ignition against the anchor fields, Seln simply *absent* from every sightline that matters, and Cael — the public suite spent openly, banking long dead — inside the Anchor specialist's closing geometry for four sustained exchanges, at survival intensity, ultimate stakes, an architecture in earnest adversarial use against him at contact range. The conditions the model requires, complete. The B4 Ch9 line, drawn in advance and stood on for three books, permits exactly this. He reaches. Directed. Open-eyed. Mid-fight — and unbinds her own fixed point from inside it, stepping through a gap her Path says cannot exist. The team breaks off the moment the geometry fails — deniable operations do not fight losing engagements — and is gone with a professionalism that is its own signature. The notice arrives on the road, and the page shows it in full:
