   242	**Chapter 15 — The Cache**
   243	~4,600 words
   244	
   245	The revelation, first movement — the locked case opened, on the page, after two books of the reader watching it stay shut. The annex: Seln's chosen ground, sightlines controlled, one lamp, the six of them. He opens the case without preamble — a keeper's economy, no speech — and the chapter stages the contents physically before intellectually, because the series trusts objects: fifteen years of an intelligence officer's private duplicates. Case files with reclassification orders clipped to Kindling records that do not match them. Field reports on practitioners — some of them names Seln was assigned to watch — whose demonstrated architecture exceeded their filed Path by margins no honest evaluation survives. Registry memoranda, copies of copies, a lineage of administrative language two centuries deep, all orbiting one recurring phrase in the oldest stratum: *corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.* And the artifact at the case's floor, in a wax-paper sleeve older than everything above it: a protocol-era maintenance directive fragment — genuine, provenance documented in Seln's own cipher — referencing the Arbiter evaluation sub-layer's *advisory corrections function*.
   350	
   351	**Chapter 23 — The Registry Line**
   352	~4,600 words
   353	
   354	The crossing — the road chapter, staged with the series' full weight because the series has pointed at this boundary since a fourteen-year-old was walked out of Denvash through a back exit: the east road out of Ostrand's province, four days, the country thinning — and the infrastructure thinning with it, which the chapter makes Cael catalogue because cataloguing is how he loves things: the last chartered waystation; the last posted tier-verification notice; the last watcher, who holds at the provincial boundary marker like a man at the end of a leash, logs them through his glass, and — the book's driest grace note — offers Brom a nod, professional to professional, before turning back. Then the last Arbiter station: a small, old, well-kept post at a river crossing, the sigil dark in its housing, and the six of them pass it one at a time, each given a single sentence of interiority as they do — Lira, who was measured wrong by one of these; Brom, who was priced by one; Karis, who studied them until she found their oldest stratum; Seln, who serviced the machine they feed; and Cael, last, who stops. Holds a moment at the dark sigil — the instrument that started everything, eleven seconds of silence, seven years ago — and does not perform anything at it. "You couldn't read me," he tells it, conversationally. "Turns out that was the most honest thing the system ever did." He walks on. **The Registry Line** — the practical edge of the Arbiter network, the boundary asset-restriction cannot cross because there is no registry beyond it to execute in — passes under the cart's wheels at mid-afternoon, unmarked, and the chapter refuses to dramatize the meter of road where the continent's whole apparatus of measurement simply ends. Lira is the one who says it, an hour later, realizing: "Nobody's watching." Four books of rotations, watchers, windows, files — and an hour of road where the sentence is simply true.
   355	
   356	The Daeva deferral, paid here with weight: the night camp, and Cael answers her letter — the book shows his reply in full, short, in his register: *They ruled. We left. You said keep myself unclassified — done, permanently, on my own paper this time. The rematch stands. Not postponed: relocated. It happens on a floor nobody owns. I think you'd prefer that anyway. — C.* The letter goes back west with a waystation courier at the Line — the last piece of mail the registry's world will carry for him — and the log banks the thread beside its lineage: "Reydan wanted the answer when I have it. Daeva wants the rematch with whatever I am. Both promises ride east with me. Both get kept. Not yet."
