   380	- [ ] **The challenge staged per bible, exactly:** Filed as the resolution of B5's threat-classification proceedings (Ch1); would remove the demonstration-provision enrollment and compel Compact re-evaluation (Ch2, ruled Ch18); the re-evaluation is unpassable because the Compact cannot evaluate [SHATTERED] architecture, and the failed-evaluation procedure is expulsion and asset-restriction (Ch2, read into the record Ch18). The B4 checkpoint's stratum cross-check honored on the page: the assay clause bypasses Arbiter evaluation, the falsification lives inside it, and the challenge's function is to herd Cael back into the owned stratum (Karis's half-knowledge Ch2, full payoff Ch16). The internal arc runs pole to pole: *I can work within the system* written in confidence Ch2, cracked on the Ostrand road Ch13, landed intellectually with the cache Ch16, landed finally at the fire Ch24 — and the book's discipline holds throughout: the defense never loses an argument; it loses the premise.
   381	- [ ] **The revelation staged per bible, exactly:** Seln provides his intelligence cache — the files collected during his years as a Compact operative, carried since he walked away from the job (his functional defection, B4, honored as the referent) — opened Ch15, analyzed Ch16. Contents: two hundred years of falsified classifications; politically inconvenient abilities reclassified into lesser Paths at Kindling; the mechanism a modification built into the Arbiter evaluation protocol itself, so old no current official knows it's there. The Level 4 flag payoff staged explicitly: the flag is the corrections function's contingency for the architecture it cannot falsify; Ilsev's referral returns are the sub-layer defending itself; her Ch18 testimony enters the returns' text into public record and Karis's Ch16 analysis names the connection for the reader ("filing its answers"). Ilsev and Havel remain unknowing carriers — their notes pay off for the READER, not to them; both preserved intact for later books (Ilsev filing to the letter; Havel transferred out, note in hand, six entries).
   382	- [ ] **Vastin's moment staged per bible, exactly:** Ch17 — the companions bring him the cache; the trust-increments of B4–B5 (advisory, attendance, warning) are the stated basis for the risk; he is silent for a long time — the series' longest held silence, staged in real time; then the bible's line VERBATIM: *"I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be."* He does NOT switch sides — no alliance, no files shared, no walkout participation, asked directly and answering "No" on the page — and he STOPS being the Compact's instrument, staged in four concrete acts: standing denied and the unsolicited finding written anyway (Ch5), the cache received and not reported (Ch17 — the crossing), the re-evaluation certification refused (Ch17–18 — the withheld signature the trap wanted), the closure summary returned unannotated (Ch22 — the man who once wrote *continue observation* now writing nothing). He remains INSIDE the Compact at book's end; his departure and the classified-history material he takes when he goes are Book 8's and are not staged, hinted, or scheduled here.
   383	- [ ] **The walk-out staged per bible, exactly:** Not expelled — they RESIGN, before the compelled re-evaluation date, in good standing (Ch21), converting the machine's verb into their own. The four images verbatim: Karis forfeits her ranking (Iron R3 + standing, line-itemed at Bracken's counter, Ch21); Lira walks out of the last class she'll ever take at a formal institution (mid-session, on her feet, Ch21); Brom carries three boxes of Karis's compiled materials (three trips, help refused, Ch21 — with the B5 pending-advancement-review thread paid off in Ch19: advancement FOUND, guidance-note priced at Cael, never answered — the review is what the boxes cost him and the chapter stages the exchange rate in his own four-word arithmetic); Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning (Ch22, no decision scene, no goodbye, keys squared, the case gone). Withrow does not stop them and banks her thread ("make the record say why"). Ephram stays — his arc complete (Ch20), his staying framed as right for him.
   384	- [ ] **The ending staged per bible, exactly:** The companions in the edge territories for the first time (Ch23–24), beyond the Registry Line — no institutional backing, no ranking protection, and the Compact file updated in the closing faceless window with the bible's phrase VERBATIM: *active non-compliance, observation priority escalated* (Ch24). Cael's fragment log contains NINE integrated abilities (Ch24, shown complete). He still doesn't have a tier — and the book's last movement holds the Arc 3 promise (the edge territories as the first place he will be fully free) without pre-paying it: no Quieting, no Oryn, no ruin sites, no Path-dead zones anywhere in this book. The Ch23–24 geography is built as Book 7's literal on-ramp and nothing more.
   385	- [ ] **Fragment count at book close:** 9 confirmed integrations — Wind-adjacent (B1, Lira), Pressure-adjacent (B1, Feryn), Iron-adjacent (B2 Ch13, Brom), Compression-adjacent (B2 Ch24, Reydan), Ember-adjacent (B3 Ch13, Karis), Shadow-adjacent (B4 Ch13, Seln), Storm-adjacent (B5 Ch21, Daeva; stability flag cleared B6 Ch1), **Anchor-adjacent (B6 Ch13, unnamed Compact operative — first Silver-equivalent notice, first nameless source)**, **Blade-adjacent (B6 Ch20, Ephram — first *earnest — non-hostile* engagement field)**. Plus one anomaly entry (Tide-adjacent, B2 Ch19) — referenced ONLY in Ch14 and Ch24 inventories with the standing three words, plus the single mandated non-connection check in Ch16 ("Not this either. Still open."); NOT reproduced, NOT confirmed, NOT advanced, never counted as a tenth entry. Trajectory check against bible: Book 6 close requires nine integrated abilities — DELIVERED exactly; Book 7's first acquisition (Tide-adjacent diagnostic, per the bible's Oryn material) will be the anomaly's long-deferred convergence and is NOT anticipated here beyond the anomaly staying open. Both acquisitions honor the B3/B4 ethics machinery: Anchor via the classical adversarial-combat conditions; Blade via the earnest-engagement refinement with the cost logged in advance per the B4 Ch9 covenant, and with Ephram never learning the mechanism.
   386	- [ ] **Companion status at book close:** Lira — Wind Path, Iron-tier Rank 1 formal (certification survives resignation; enrollment doesn't); Silver trajectory declined on-page at full price (Ch10 built it, Ch19 spent it); out. Brom — Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier formal FOREVER (the advancement found and never certified — the number his family wanted, left on a table in Ostrand); continental Copper champion, permanent; out. Karis — Ember Path, ranking FORFEITED on-page (was Iron R3); her compiled research travels as three boxes / the field archive; out. Seln — Shadow Path, Bronze-tier; defected in FORM at last (the desk, the personnel query, the review-flagged fifteen years); formally inside the circle from Ch15 (mechanism known, debt paid); the cache travels with the companions. Ephram — arc complete (Ch20, ninth fragment source, "still glad"); remains at Halcenvane, cohort captain, available to later books as the inside friend. Hesk — letter beats only; the Denvash asset-mapping (Ch7) is a letter beat and stays one; his full history remains OPEN. All six travelers positioned exactly where Book 7 Chapter 1 needs them: the edge territories, first camp, no employer.
   387	- [ ] **Antagonist status at book close:** The Guilds Compact — challenge filed, sustained, ruled, and MOOTED by resignation; asset-restriction *pending jurisdiction* (armed, unexecutable beyond the Line — a standing threat for any return west, deliberately kept live); the file's closing designation is the bible's verbatim phrase; the falsification machinery UNDISTURBED and UNAWARE it has been documented (nothing was filed; the sub-layer was never probed; the Compact's institutional knowledge of the cache's contents is ZERO, though Seln's disappearance has ignited a quiet review of what he kept — deliberate open dread, available to Books 7–9). The faceless faction — Vastin's "people who want this to stop" — moved twice (escort order Ch11, road interception Ch13), stayed faceless throughout (Jent is counsel, not face; the presiding seat is a seal; the operatives are nameless), and remains UNNAMED and UNLOCATED: open into Arc 3. Vastin — no longer the instrument, not an ally, still inside; unresolved by design (B8's material). Ilsev — proceedings executed to the letter, the returns' text now in public record; post-ruling state open. Havel — transferred out of monitoring, six entries, carrier preserved. Coss — offstage entirely (at most one passing mention); grade still uncapped. Warden-rank enforcement never appears — the book's escalation is deliberately *not* wardens; it is paper and namelessness, per Vastin's B5 warning.
   388	- [ ] **Naming — screened against the full B1/B2/B3/B4/B5 collision registry:** New names this book are DELIBERATELY MINIMAL — the antagonist is faceless by design, and the naming surface enforces the theme. New: **Procurator Jent** (Compact counsel — screened vs. Joren: J-o vs. J-e, distinct shape and sound; vs. Yeni: distinct; chosen AFTER rejecting "Rask" for Hesk-rhyme (-sk ending on a high-frequency name), "Pryce" for Prynn-adjacency (P-r-y), "Ulmer" for the Ulric/Umber U-family, and "Emmert" for Ember-collision), **Anchor Path** (new Path name — chosen AFTER rejecting "Ward Path" for root-collision with the Warden rank (Coss's title); screened vs. Shield Path: distinct in kind (cages the other vs. protects the self) and the distinction stated in the reference section), **the Registry Line** and **the Compact's Hand** (coined terms, descriptive register, zero collision surface). Unnamed BY DESIGN (the old-yard-owner convention, deployed structurally): the presiding registrar (a seal, not a person), the Anchor specialist and the off-channel teams (the first nameless fragment source — thematic), Hesk's registry visitors, Lira's Silver opponents, the morning clerk. Standing rules held: no new D-names (Daeva remains the sanctioned sole exception, closed after B5), no new S-names near Seln, no new V-names near Vastin/Vell/Velmere, no new H-a/H-el names, no C-r additions, no -essa names, no new -mere places, no bare "Iron Path" character anywhere near Brom ("Iron Skin" spelled in full at every occurrence; the Ch19 advancement finding says "Iron-tier" of a formal tier, which is the registry's term and not a character). Reserved names untouched: **Oryn (B7)**. No Tide Path practitioner is cast, named, or present anywhere in this book.
   389	- [ ] **PROVISIONAL facts used:** Anchor Path and its mechanics — PROVISIONAL, defined this book; Books 7+ must match (fixed-point binding, lattice-perception, enforcement-service association). The classification-challenge machinery, asset-restriction procedure, pre-restriction inventory, and the Registry Line's jurisdictional logic — PROVISIONAL worldbuilding, built consistent with B3's registry-law texture, B4's assay-clause stratum, and the bible's expulsion/asset-restriction sentence; Book 7+ should treat the Line as the standing legal boundary and *pending jurisdiction* as the live threat any westward return re-arms. The cache's specific contents (reclassification orders, memoranda lineage, the maintenance fragment and its *advisory corrections function*) — PROVISIONAL in detail, LOCKED in finding; Book 8's Architect material must sit BENEATH this stratum (the corrections function is the two-century modification; the sub-layer it rides in is older and unattributed — B8's reveal slots under it without contradiction, and B8 architecture should cross-check the Ch16 strata language when the Architect's protocol is dated). Jent, the escort-order paper chain, and the off-channel teams' provenance — PROVISIONAL, new this book, deliberately under-explained.
   390	- [ ] **LOCKED threads verified untouched or correctly maintained:** Tide-adjacent anomaly — inventory references (Ch14, Ch24), three words, one mandated non-connection line (Ch16), no Tide practitioner cast (stated drafting constraint); explicitly NOT the falsification, explicitly unexplained by it. [UNBOUND] — ONE controlled appearance (Ch16): the strata-dating advance (renaming era = falsification era, same authors) and the stated limit (the word, the layer's base, and the old danger all beyond the cache's reach); no meaning revealed, LOCKED until Book 8; the two mysteries and the discharged SECRET are kept explicitly distinct on the page in Karis's method-voice ("the middle of the story, not the beginning"). Daeva's rematch — touched with full weight (Ch6 statement + letter) and explicitly DEFERRED (Ch23–24, "relocated," logged beside Reydan's); LOCKED open, warm. Iron Skin watcher, market stranger, Hesk's history (letters only), Vell's session (letter reference at most, Ch24), Coss's grade — untouched. Records-broker residue (the circulating dossier) — background texture only. NEW open threads created deliberately: the faceless faction (cold, B7+ pressure), the nameless operative (log-texture debt), Havel's transfer, Ilsev's after, Withrow's ledger, the sub-layer's base stratum (the B8 bridge), and the Compact's dawning question about what Seln kept (dread, unhurried).
   391	- [ ] **The internal arc staged honestly at both poles:** *I can work within the system* written in the log, in confidence, with three books of receipts, Chapter 2; defended at council against Lira's honest challenge, Chapter 9; cracked on the Ostrand road, Chapter 13 ("the room was never the system"); landed intellectually in the annex, Chapter 16 ("It isn't broken. It's *maintained*."); landed finally, without triumphalism, at the fire, Chapter 24. The book's structural discipline carries the argument mechanically: the defense wins every argument (Ch8's sustained-validity, Ch10's Jent admission, Ch18's masterwork closing) and the machine proceeds anyway — the reader is never allowed to believe a better brief would have saved them, because the corridor was never an argument. And the walkout is earned by pricing, not by rhetoric: every stake is built (Ch4), lived in (Ch10, Ch19), and paid on the page (Ch21) — the book ends with the companions owning nothing the system gave them except each other and the truth, which is the Arc 2 exit the whole arc was for.

