+# Editor Recheck — Book 7 Architecture (r3)
+
+## Verdict: STRUCTURAL_HOLD
+
+The repair closes E03, E05, E07, and N02. E01, N01, and N03 remain partially open, and the repair introduces one new ending-state contradiction, N04. Open finding count: **4**.
+
+## Dispositions — E01, E03, E05, E07, N01, N02, N03
+
+### E01 — PARTIALLY CLOSED
+
+The construction/recipient-purpose and unbounded-architecture claims are repaired. Chapter 17 now limits Cael's statement to the observed tactical asymmetry: **“They chose a floor that makes the rest of you helpless. They don't know it doesn't do anything to me.”** Chapter 22 explicitly frames the missing banks as an epistemic limit rather than an ontological fact: **“the not-finding is described as what it is — a limit of the reading, his and hers — and not as a fact about the architecture.”** The closing discovery also stops at the permitted line: **“Someone made this pattern.”**
+
+What remains is the r2 finding's final reading/measurement problem. The repaired book-level arc says **“the oldest thing out here doesn't measure me either, and it measures everything else,”** the Protagonist Arc Statement substitutes **“doesn't read me either — and it reads everything else,”** and Chapter 16 writes **“The oldest thing out here doesn't either — and it reads everyone else at a line you can mark with a rock.”** These remain affirmative claims that the Quieting sites read or measure practitioners and selectively fail to read Cael. Book 7's locked ceiling supports only the observable facts: standard Paths and Arbiters fail at the line, Cael's fragments do not, and the sites align. Remove the remaining *read/measure* relationship from the arc/header, Chapter 16, and the closing register; do not replace it with another claim about what the sites are doing to or with Cael.
+
+### E03 — CLOSED
+
+The governing Tide reference now defines both modes and reserves whole-architecture knowledge for the long one: **“the surface reading — hands on the hurt, seconds, finds injury and the Path beneath it — and the deep reading — both hands, the healer's whole current run through the whole architecture, minutes, patient still and uninjured, at a mending's cost; whole-architecture knowledge is available ONLY from the deep reading.”** Chapter 13 runs the deep reading **“slowly, for minutes.”** Chapter 22 now keeps the refused Teague knowledge on that same side of the rule: **“not in a second; in the minutes the deep reading takes, with Teague still and willing.”** No speed/mechanism contradiction remains.
+
+### E05 — CLOSED
+
+The decisive Anchor/wyrm interface is now cold-tested before combat. Chapter 21 stages **“an Anchor-adjacent fixed point laid on the flooded floor twenty paces out”** and then shows **“the water answers at the point, and keeps answering”** until Karis concludes, **“It reads that as the heaviest thing on the floor.”** The fight pays that exact result with **“one fixed point of held pressure at the hall's broken center.”** The tenth fragment remains out of combat: **“The tenth fragment is used in no fight in this book.”**
+
+### E07 — CLOSED
+
+The Long Stair card now names the concealed use and supplies its witness logic: Cael enters with **“the suite held to nothing but one thing — Shadow-adjacent, presence thinned to nothing, movement folded into the bowl's dusk,”** and the rim team **“never perceive him, and what is never perceived cannot be attributed.”** This matches the checkpoint and preserves the public seal: the off-channel team does not witness an attributable Shadow-adjacent declaration, and Teague arrives only after the fight.
+
+### N01 — PARTIALLY CLOSED
+
+The main roster is normalized. The header now says **“Party entering the book: FIVE — Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln,”** Chapter 9 says **“Six on the road”** when Oryn joins, Chapter 23 returns to **“Five on the bearing”** after she turns north, and Chapter 24 records **“Crew of six.”**
+
+Two count defects remain:
+
+- Chapter 7 still calls Oryn **“the seventh companion given her own arc-spine,”** contradicting the repaired sixth-chair/six-person roster.
+- The Clue / Plant Ledger says the second posting targets **“five practitioners … four bodies and one anomaly,”** while Chapter 17 correctly says **“six practitioners will be five bodies and one anomaly,”** and Chapters 18–21 stage all six. The ledger sentence was introduced by this repair and is now one practitioner short.
+
+What remains: change the Chapter 7 ordinal to the document's six-person convention and normalize the trap sentence to six practitioners / five disabled Path users plus Cael.
+
+### N02 — CLOSED
+
+Oryn's reference entry now says **“She learns the mechanism in Chapter 14 (the circle's wider council is Chapter 16).”** That matches Chapter 14's **“The disclosure to Oryn,”** Chapter 16's wider circle, and the checkpoint's **“mechanism known (Ch14).”**
+
+### N03 — PARTIALLY CLOSED
+
+Chapter 11 now defines a draftable injury state: **“the arm saved”** but under Oryn's order **“not to be used to hold, strike, or catch until she says,”** with Lira fighting one-armed through first snow. Chapters 14, 17, 19, 21, and 24 consistently carry the functional restriction, and Chapter 19 gives the promised failure cause: **“the mend on Lira's forearm reopened where she caught herself on the stair.”**
+
+One state transition remains asserted only in the checkpoint. The Chapter 19 card ends its medical accounting with the reopened mend and stages Oryn mending Brom, but never stages Lira's wound being closed again. The checkpoint nevertheless says the mend **“reopened at the Stair (Ch19) and closed again outside the line.”** Add that second treatment to the Chapter 19 card, or remove the checkpoint's closure and carry the open wound consistently. Until then, the card and close-state ledger disagree.
+
+## Roster audit
+
+- **Chapter 1 — 5:** work priced for **“the five of them”; “five practitioners.”**
+- **Chapter 2 — 5:** Teague reads **“the five of them.”**
+- **Chapter 3 — 5:** **“Crew of five, four injured”** and **“four of five got hurt.”**
+- **Chapter 6 — 5 before Oryn arrives:** **“five practitioners on a mountain.”** Oryn's arrival makes six physically present at the end, without a contradictory count.
+- **Chapter 7 — 5-person crew in Teague's accounting:** **“cleared Thornwater's ford with five”** and **“lost a cart … with five.”** Miss: the card also calls Oryn **“the seventh companion.”**
+- **Chapter 8 — 5:** **“five voices.”**
+- **Chapter 9 — 6:** **“Six on the road”; “the sixth chair … with the five.”**
+- **Chapter 11 — 6 total:** the five other practitioners have their Paths back after Cael crosses; **“Four practitioners with no Paths and a healer … watched the sixth.”**
+- **Chapter 12 — 6 total:** **“five people saw”** Cael use the fragments, meaning the five witnesses besides Cael.
+- **Chapter 14 — 6:** **“the six of them”** at the birthday table.
+- **Chapter 15 — 6 total:** Cael counts **“four people who saw what I did inside, and one healer”** besides himself.
+- **Chapter 16 — 6:** **“The circle … six for the first time”** and **“six intelligent people.”**
+- **Chapter 17 — 6 roster / 5 physically at Lowmarch:** Oryn is temporarily **“three days north”** on her route; the trap is designed for the full **“six practitioners … five bodies and one anomaly.”** The separate Clue / Plant Ledger's five/four formulation is a miss.
+- **Chapter 18 — 6:** **“the six of them”** and **“the sixth chair.”**
+- **Chapter 19 — 6:** five Path-disabled party members can hold the stair while Cael remains functional; the close confirms **“a crew of six alive.”** The other six in the card are the off-channel enemy team, not the party.
+- **Chapter 20 — 6:** **“the six of them back down the stair.”**
+- **Chapter 21 — 6:** **“Six people on a wall-top.”**
+- **Chapter 23 — 6, then 5:** **“the six of them on a bearing”** before Oryn turns north; **“Five on the bearing”** and a **“council of five voices”** afterward. **“The four of you”** correctly excludes Cael from the four Path users who would be affected at the unentered site.
+- **Chapter 24 — 6 roster, 5 physically at Lowmarch:** Pike enters **“Crew of six”;** Oryn makes it **“Six, by letter”;** Cael names **“five people, one of them on a route”** besides himself.
+
+Cards 4, 5, 10, 13, and 22 contain no numerical party count. Card-level scene arithmetic otherwise confirms five through Chapter 8, six from Chapter 9 through Oryn's Chapter 23 departure, and five on the road thereafter. The Chapter 7 ordinal and non-card Clue / Plant Ledger trap count are the two misses.
+
+## New findings
+
+### N04 — The ending assigns the unentered fourth site a tested effect
+
+- **Severity:** MEDIUM
+- **Gate:** Internal event-state accuracy; Book 7 ending evidence ceiling.
+- **Evidence:** Chapter 23 says of the fourth site, **“Nobody crosses the line.”** Chapter 24 correctly summarizes the party as having **“walked into the quiet three times and come out.”** The repair then adds: **“Four floors older than any registry had let every Path in the party die at a line and let his stand.”** Only the Fallow Ring, Long Stair, and Drowned Hall were entered and tested. The fourth site's doubled perimeter was paced from outside; no Path failure or Cael comparison occurred there.
+- **Consequence:** The final narration converts an untested fourth site into observed evidence and contradicts the same chapter's three-entry count. It also compounds E01's agency problem through **“had let.”**
+- **Repair target:** Keep the four-site alignment but limit the functional evidence to the three entered sites: at three sites the other Paths failed and Cael's fragments did not; the fourth was sighted, paced, cordoned, and not crossed.
+
+## Strengths preserved
+
+- The Book 7 series deliveries remain intact: independent edge-territory work, Oryn's route-based arrival, the Fallow Ring encounter, the Tide-adjacent non-combat acquisition, and the four-site alignment all have full card support.
+- The repaired Tide rules are unusually clean: surface and deep readings now have distinct duration, information, cost, and patient-state constraints, and the Teague refusal pays the ethics thread without cheating the mechanism.
+- The Drowned Hall fight now passes the mechanism-before-payoff test. The afternoon fixed-point trial makes the Anchor solution legible while preserving the wyrm's animal logic and keeping Tide-adjacent out of combat.
+- The Long Stair now preserves both tactical clarity and the Shadow public seal. The use is explicit to the drafting team, concealed in-world, unattributable to the rim team, and unseen by Teague.
+- Lira's Chapter 11 outcome is substantially stronger than in r2: the arm is saved rather than magically restored, the seasonal load restriction is concrete, and the Stair reopening pays the warning. Only the unstaged second closure remains.
+- Oryn's disclosure timeline, professional differentiation, independent route, and consent condition remain coherent and distinctive.
+- The opening five-person cards and the six-person cards after Chapter 9 are now overwhelmingly consistent; Chapter 23's six-to-five transition is explicit and correct.
+- The strongest future plants remain observational: identical Arbiter failure at the boundary, Cael's already-dark Arbiter, survey stakes and map blanks, Karis's controlled strata limit, and the alignment discovered on paper.

